A lambda expression may no longer mention `allocated(e)` in its body, precondition or reads clause, except inside an `old` expression within the lambda. The verifier evaluated such a lambda in the heap it is applied in, so its value depended on that heap's allocation state, while the frame axiom of its arrow type treats its value as fixed by its reads frame; with an empty reads frame, `(x: int) => allocated(c)` applied after `c` was allocated proved `false`. Such a use is now a resolution error, as it already was in functions and constants. The hidden option `--legacy-allocated-in-lambdas` restores the old, unsound rule, for comparison and migration only.
